I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the framework guidance first, then identify candidate phases and carry the best structural model through GSAS-II refinement. I’ll keep the raw inputs untouched and validate that every reported fraction and profile comes from the saved `final.gpx`.

The chemistry suggests a partially reacted CaCO₃/WO₃ system at 500 °C, so I’ll test the target tungstate and plausible residual/intermediate phases instead of assuming a single CaWO₄ product. The framework also confirms that specimen displacement, roughness, size/strain, texture, and reference-coordinate mismatch may all be present, so phase identification must precede flexible profile refinement.

Peak positions now give a clean three-phase assignment: tetragonal scheelite CaWO₄, monoclinic room-temperature WO₃, and calcite CaCO₃. The narrow calcite lines independently trace the angle-dependent specimen shift, while the broader CaWO₄/WO₃ peaks establish phase-specific size/strain broadening. I’m moving to whole-pattern trials to select the best WO₃ reference setting and quantify whether any fourth crystalline phase is warranted.

The initial refinement reaches about 12.4% Rwp before coordinate or texture modeling, confirming that the three identified phases explain the major profile. I also found and removed a redundant histogram-scale degree of freedom; the current parallel trials compare five WO₃ models under identical, non-singular refinement settings.

The WO₃ comparison is computationally heavier than the initial single run because each candidate is being fully re-profiled, not just stick-pattern matched. I’m letting the identical trials finish so the model choice is based on profile residuals and refined physical parameters rather than database ranking.

The candidate jobs remain active and are CPU-bound rather than stalled. While they run, the key qualitative discriminator is already consistent with monoclinic γ-WO₃: the observed 23.42/23.90/24.67° triplet and its intensity ordering reject the short-cell early WO₃ model; the remaining comparison is among closely related room-temperature refinements.

The whole-pattern comparison decisively selects the modern monoclinic/triclinic WO₃ framework family: orthorhombic and short-cell alternatives fit much worse. The two best cells collapse to the same pseudo-monoclinic metric; I’m now comparing them with coordinate refinement, where the lower-symmetry triclinic model has many extra parameters and must show a meaningful, physically plausible gain to be preferred.

The quantitative three-phase solution is currently stable near 12% CaWO₄, 59% WO₃, and 29% calcite by mass before final coordinate/profile corrections. Those are provisional because preferred orientation and surface roughness can bias scale factors; I’ll report uncertainties beyond the formal least-squares errors if those effects remain correlated.

